 #md "# ADR-2606162200: .md.edn — markdown as commented EDN

**Status**: accepted
**Date**: 2026-06-16
**Deciders**: Jun Kawasaki

# Context

ADR docs are Markdown with YAML front matter; the machine index lives separately in
`90-docs/adr/adr-index.edn` — a 2.8 MB **single-line** EDN blob that is effectively
un-hand-editable, hides corruption in a 1-line diff, and races badly in the shared
checkout (the same failure class `deps.edn` hit, ADR-2606121143). YAML front matter is
also untyped (`true`/`5.5`/dates are strings) and supports no comments or form-discard.

The question: *can a single file be both the human doc AND a commented, typed,
machine-readable record?* A true polyglot (renders as Markdown **and** parses as EDN
for arbitrary prose) is not achievable — EDN's top level is one form, so free prose
breaks parsing. But the inverse works cleanly.

# Decision

Adopt **`.md.edn`** as an optional, content-equivalent representation of an ADR:

- The file is **one valid EDN map**. Metadata are **typed EDN** keys
  (`:authoritative true`, `:priority 5.5`, `:depends-on [\"adr-…\"]`), so it supports
  `;;` line comments and `#_` form-discard.
- The prose body is a **`#md \"…\"` tagged string** — markdown verbatim, only `\"` and
  `\\` escaped, newlines kept literal so it stays readable.
- **`.md.edn` is canonical; the `.md` is a generated render** for GitHub display. A
  bidirectional bridge, `etzhayyim.tools.adr-mdedn`, converts both ways and is
  round-trip-exact on the DATA (metadata map + body), normalizing only incidental
  whitespace:

      .md  ──md->data──▶ {meta + :body} ──emit-mdedn──▶ .md.edn   (canonical)
      .md.edn ─read-mdedn─▶ {meta + :body} ──mdedn->md──▶ .md       (render)

- bb tasks: `bb adr:to-mdedn <*.md>` (migrate) and `bb adr:render <*.md.edn>`
  (regenerate the .md). This ADR is itself authored as `.md.edn` and its `.md` is
  generated — the format dogfoods itself.

Markdown stays the canonical *human* source per `90-docs/CLAUDE.edn`; `.md.edn` is the
typed, comment-bearing sibling.

**Index generation (landed in this ADR).** `bb adr:index` regenerates
`90-docs/adr/adr-index.edn` from every ADR's front matter (`.md` YAML or `.md.edn`),
as a **canonical one-entry-per-line, clojure.edn-parseable** file — replacing the
hand-edited 2.8 MB single-line blob that *did not even parse* (`Map literal must contain
an even number of forms` — the deps.edn corruption class). The regeneration is provably
safe: it covers all 848 ADR files (vs the old 750), drops 87 **orphan** entries that
pointed at non-existent files (0 entries with a real file were lost), and ships a
`bb adr:index-check` drift gate (fails when an ADR's front matter and the index diverge)
for lefthook. `adr-index.edn` is henceforth GENERATED, never hand-edited.

# Consequences

- A doc's metadata and prose live in **one** file, typed and commentable, hand-editable
  and merge-friendly (unlike the single-line index blob).
- Cost: GitHub does not render `.md.edn`, so the `.md` must be regenerated on change
  (a lefthook `--check` gate that fails on drift is the recommended enforcement).
- `etzhayyim.tools.adr-mdedn` ships with 8 tests / 25 assertions; verified round-trip on
  the real `2606162030` ADR (data-equal after both emit and render).
- No docs-system invariant is amended; YAML-front-matter `.md` remains valid and
  supported. Adoption is opt-in per ADR.

# Alternatives Considered

- **Embed nothing; keep YAML + hand-edited index.** Rejected: the single-line
  `adr-index.edn` is the exact pain this addresses.
- **True Markdown/EDN polyglot.** Rejected as infeasible for arbitrary prose (EDN top
  level is one form).
- **EDN front matter inside a `.md`** (option B): keeps GitHub rendering, parses the
  front-matter block only. Viable and complementary, but the user chose the fully-EDN
  `.md.edn` (option A) so the *whole* file is one commented EDN value.
- **Line-split the index only** (the `deps.edn` treatment, ADR-2606121143). Helps the
  index but leaves doc+metadata split across two files; subsumed once the index is
  generated from `.md.edn`.

